#version 450
// Direct 2-D convolution, stride 1, symmetric zero padding, weights in
// PyTorch's [OC, IC, KH, KW] order. Covers both shapes the decoder uses:
// 3x3 pad 1 and the 1x1 pad 0 residual shortcuts.
//
// Each thread owns one output pixel and OC_BLOCK output channels, so the
// activation window it reads is multiplied into OC_BLOCK accumulators held
// in registers. The grid is (spatial tiles, output-channel blocks) and each
// workgroup stages its slice of the filter through shared memory, a block of
// input channels at a time.
//
// The register blocking is the load-bearing part, and it is IDEAS §2.1's
// result applied to a different operator. Profiling the first version of
// this decoder put conv3x3 at 90.3% of the whole decode at ~750 GFLOP/s,
// 3% of this part's measured 22.9 TFLOP/s fp32 FMA ceiling. Staging the
// weights in shared memory -- i.e. fixing the *weight* traffic -- moved that
// only to ~850, which is what said the weights were never the problem: the
// kernel re-reads each activation once per output channel, and the fix is to
// raise arithmetic intensity by computing several channels per read rather
// than to move bytes closer.
//
// Input-channel blocking keeps the LDS footprint off the occupancy: the full
// 512-channel filter for eight output channels would be 147 KB, a 32-channel
// block of it is 9 KB.
//
// Both blocks are compile-time (IMAGE.md Q9b), because 8 was never screened
// past the point where it beat 1 and the argument above does not stop at 8:
// one activation load still feeds only OC_BLOCK fused multiply-adds, so the
// loads per FLOP -- which is what binds this kernel, not the weights and not
// the arithmetic -- fall as 1/OC_BLOCK the whole way. Qwen's decoder measured
// the default at 3.2 TFLOP/s against a ~15 TFLOP/s LDS-bound ceiling (one
// shared read per multiply-add is the term that does not fall), and the arms
// are screened in qimage/vae rather than assumed. Raising OC_BLOCK changes no
// accumulator's summation order -- the loops over input channel and tap are
// untouched -- so every arm's output is *bit-identical*, which is what the
// screen asserts.
//
// REFLECT builds the same filter over PyTorch's "reflect" padding instead of
// zeros (MiniMax-H3's video encoder, VIDEO.md M10): a tap at -1 reads row 1
// and a tap at H reads row H-2. It is only meaningful for pad <= 1, which is
// every filter that build runs.
//
// EPILOGUE builds the same filter with TAEQI2.1's tail fused into the store
// (qimage/vae/tiny_gpu.go): aux0 bit 0 adds the tensor at resOff, bit 1
// applies a ReLU after it. That covers everything the tiny decoder does
// between convolutions -- conv+ReLU, and a block's conv+skip+ReLU -- so its
// graph is convolutions and upsamples only. Without the define the store is
// the plain one, so the full decoder's builds are unchanged.
//
// IC_BLOCK trades LDS footprint against barrier count and changes nothing
// numerically either; the products are still accumulated in ascending channel
// order.

#include "vae_common.glsl"

layout(local_size_x = 256) in;

#ifndef OC_BLOCK
#define OC_BLOCK 8u
#endif
#version 450
// The mid block's projections as a tiled fp32 GEMM -- IMAGE.md Q9b.
//
// It replaces shaders/vae_linear.comp on Qwen's VAE, where the profiler put
// those four 1152x1152 projections at **1.46 s of a 7.35 s decode, 19.9%, at
// 35 GFLOP/s**. z-image reached for shaders/dit_gemm.comp at this point and
// got 62 GFLOP/s -> matrix cores; that route is closed here, because
// qimage/vae's TestConvFP16Ladder measures an fp16 operand *anywhere* in this
// decoder at max abs ~0.09 on the decoded image against the fp32 path's
// 7.3e-4 -- the tail norm divides a per-pixel L2 out of a residual stream
// running at absmax 2.6e5, so a relative perturbation upstream arrives as an
// absolute one at a quiet pixel. So the arithmetic stays fp32 and what gets
// fixed is the shape.
//
// What was wrong with the naive kernel is the weight traffic, and it is the
// same fault the conv kernel had before its register blocking: one thread per
// output element reads a whole weight row from global, so the [OC, K] weight
// is re-read once per row of A -- 21.7 GB per projection at 4096 rows.
//
// So: one workgroup owns a BM x BN tile of the output, 256 threads each hold
// a TM x TN register tile, and a BK-deep slab of both operands is staged in
// shared memory per step. The weight is then read once per *tile* of rows
// rather than once per row -- 64x less -- and each thread does TM*TN = 16
// multiply-adds per TM+TN = 8 shared reads, against the conv kernel's one per
// one.
//
// **Bit-identical to vae_linear.comp, which is what the screen asserts.**
// Every accumulator still starts from the bias and still adds its products in
// ascending k, because the k loop is split into slabs but never reordered or
// split across threads. Nothing about the port moves a number; it moves bytes.
//
// A, C and the bias are fp32 in the activation arena; W is the PyTorch
// [OC, K] weight in the fp32 weight arena, read exactly as vae_linear reads
// it. Rows are pc.H * pc.W, K is pc.C, N is pc.OC.

#include "vae_common.glsl"

#define BM 64
#define BN 64
#define BK 16
#define TM 4
#define TN 4

layout(local_size_x = 256) in;

// k-major, so that the sixteen threads sharing a row group broadcast one
// address out of shA while their shB reads walk consecutive banks.
shared float shA[BK][BM];
shared float shB[BK][BN];

